 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T21:28+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether you should wear a mask outside in Singapore depends on your health status and how long you plan to be outdoors, as Singapore is currently experiencing transboundary haze with the 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) remaining in the **unhealthy to very unhealthy** range. \n\nThe Ministry of Health (MOH) and National Environment Agency (NEA) guidelines recommend the following:\n\n* **For short commutes or general errands:** Healthy individuals generally do not need to wear a mask for brief outdoor movements (such as walking to transport or running quick errands). \n* **If you are spending several hours outdoors:** If you must engage in prolonged outdoor activities, you should consider wearing a well-fitted **N95 mask** (or equivalent, such as a KN95, KF94, or FFP2 mask). \n* **Vulnerable groups:** If you are elderly, pregnant, a child, or have chronic heart or lung conditions (like asthma), you are more sensitive to the haze. You should minimize outdoor physical exertion and wear an N95-equivalent mask if you need to be outside for extended periods. \n    * *Note:* Anyone with severe heart or lung problems who experiences breathing difficulties while using an N95 mask should consult a doctor or take a break from wearing it.\n\n**What you should do next:**\n* Check the real-time **1-hour PM2.5 concentrations** and **24-hour PSI readings** on the [myENV app](https://www.nea.gov.sg/services/myenv) or [haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg) before heading out to get the most accurate, up-to-date readings for your specific region.\n* Reduce heavy outdoor physical exertion where possible.",
